Recover the first frame's timestamp instead of dropping the frame.

Every software and VAAPI subscription discards exactly one frame at start-up:

    01:H264: [mpeg2video => libx264]: Invalid pts (-9223372036854775808) <= last (0), dropping frame
    01:H264: [mpeg2video => libx264]: encode error 1/50

-9223372036854775808 is INT64_MIN, i.e. AV_NOPTS_VALUE. Across a full test run
every one of the 14 firings of that guard was an untimed frame; not one was a
genuine ordering violation.

Root cause, from trace-level logs. av_parser_parse2() emits the first frame of
a stream with no timestamps at all, even though the packet fed to it carried
both:

    before av_parser_parse2  pkt->pkt_dts 68709      pkt->pkt_pts 77718
    after  av_parser_parse2  out_pkt->pkt_dts <unset>  out_pkt->pkt_pts <unset>

Every later packet passes through intact (71712 -> 71712). The decoder then
produces a frame whose pts is AV_NOPTS_VALUE but whose dts still holds a usable
value, and the monotonicity guard in tvh_video_context_encode() reads INT64_MIN
as "older than everything" and throws the frame away:

    Decoded frame: pts=-9223372036854775808, dts=77718, duration=0
    Decoded frame: pts=80721,                dts=80721, duration=0
    Decoded frame: pts=83724,                dts=83724, duration=0

The recovered dts is not an approximation: 77718 + 3003 = 80721 exactly, 3003
being one frame at 30000/1001 in 90 kHz units. So fill the missing pts from the
dts before the guard runs.

Caveat, and the reason this is worth testing before trusting. On the QSV path
the same frame carries pts and dts in *different* time bases -- vpp_qsv outputs
in 1001/30000 while dts stays in 1/90000, giving pts=13 alongside dts=51580.
Copying dts into pts there would be wrong. It is currently unreachable: the
mpeg2_qsv hardware decoder has pipeline latency and absorbs the untimed first
frame, so no QSV frame reaches this point with pts unset (0 firings of the
guard across the QSV run). The substitution is therefore correct on every path
that can actually trigger it, but it is a latent hazard if that ever changes.

Confirmed on the reporting hardware across all three paths. The substituted
value is exact every time -- one frame at 30000/1001 is 3003 ticks at 90 kHz:

    sub    profile           first pkt   using dts   next frame pts
    0008   hevc-vaapi        I           106264      109267
    0009   hevc (software)   B            33720       36723

and no "Invalid pts" fired anywhere. QSV never triggered the substitution on
any of its attempts, so the time-base hazard above stayed unreachable in
practice.

The defect is intermittent rather than per-path: it depends on whether an
untimed frame actually surfaces from the decoder at the moment the subscription
joins the stream. A VAAPI run that joined on a P frame produced no untimed
frame at all -- the decoder had no reference and discarded everything until the
next key frame, by which point timestamps were sound again.

Still worth carrying alongside 107 and 108, which close the audio/video
start-up gap downstream in globalheaders.c. They do not overlap: this one acts
on a frame entering the encoder, they act on packets leaving for the client.
Without it the first picture lands one frame later, so 107 and 108 discard
3003 ticks more audio than they need to. See 106-108-av-start-HOWTO.txt.

Not introduced by PR #2117: the guard is identical in master (45cbe4adb).

Left alone deliberately: self->pts is seeded to 0 by the calloc of TVHContext,
and 0 is a legitimate timestamp, so a stream whose first frame genuinely starts
there would also be discarded. Fixing that means seeding with AV_NOPTS_VALUE,
which is not safe here -- tvh_audio_context_decode() shares the field and
computes "drift = new_pts - prev_pts", which would overflow from INT64_MIN. It
needs a video-only "first frame seen" flag instead, which is a wider change
than this.

--- src/transcoding/transcode/video.c.orig
+++ src/transcoding/transcode/video.c
@@ -598,6 +598,20 @@ tvh_video_context_encode(TVHContext *self, AVFrame *avframe)
             avframe->pts, avframe->pkt_dts, *frame_duration);
     }
 
+    /* av_parser_parse2() emits the first frame of a stream with no timestamps
+       at all -- both pts and dts come back unset even though the packet fed to
+       it carried them -- so the decoder hands us a frame whose pts is
+       AV_NOPTS_VALUE while its dts still holds the right value. Since
+       AV_NOPTS_VALUE is INT64_MIN, the monotonicity test below reads such a
+       frame as older than everything and discards it. Take the dts instead:
+       measured on a 30000/1001 source the recovered value was 77718 and the
+       next frame arrived at 80721, exactly one frame later. */
+    if (avframe->pts == AV_NOPTS_VALUE && avframe->pkt_dts != AV_NOPTS_VALUE) {
+        tvh_context_log(self, LOG_DEBUG,
+                        "frame has no pts, using dts (%"PRId64")", avframe->pkt_dts);
+        avframe->pts = avframe->pkt_dts;
+    }
+
     if (avframe->pts <= self->pts) {
         tvh_context_log(self, LOG_WARNING,
                         "Invalid pts (%"PRId64") <= last (%"PRId64"), dropping frame",
